Cactus Code Thorn TestPersistentGroups
Author(s)    : Liwei Ji
Maintainer(s): Liwei Ji
Licence      : LGPL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Purpose

Test that refined-level coarse-fine ghosts of checkpointed grid functions
that the time integrator does not own are refreshed by SYNC under
subcycling, for a default-tagged group (evolved by default) and for a
checkpoint="yes" evolve="no" group. Each group holds a time stamp written on
the interior every step; a check counts the points that hold neither the
level's own stamp nor, on coarse-fine ghosts, the parent level's stamp.

A third group, persist_history, has two timelevels and is declared
persistent (checkpoint="yes" evolve="no"). It is updated only occasionally,
every P = 2 * (level 0's step) finest-level steps, at times when all levels
are aligned; in between, CycleTimelevels seeds its current timelevel from
the past one. Its (tl0, tl1) pair is checked against values computed from
the level's time: tl0 is the time of the last update, and tl1 is the update
before that right after an update (and at initial), a seeded copy of tl0
otherwise. This tells a genuine past timelevel from a copy of the current
one, through cycling, moving regrids, and checkpoint/recovery. It is the
case MHD_Diagnostics::vels_old handles by hand with a single timelevel and a
reset at POSTREGRID. Validity is checked through the READS clauses: tl0
everywhere and tl1 on the interior at every check, and tl1 everywhere during
evolution.

The tests run on top of TestSubcyclingMC2's RK4 evolution with a moving
refined box (regridded every 4 iterations): moving.par uninterrupted, and
checkpoint-moving.par / recover-moving.par as a checkpoint/recover pair with
a mid-cycle checkpoint between two regrids. After recovery the tested groups
are SYNCed and checked before evolution continues.

Checkpoints carry the interior only, and SYNC does not cover the oldest
timelevel, so after recovery the past timelevel of persist_history is valid
on the interior only until the level's next step. A regrid before that step
drops it, on that level and on the finer levels prolongated from it. In
recover-moving.par level 0 does not step between the recovery and the next
regrid, so check_past_after_regrid = no skips the past timelevel in that
regrid's check.
